ain .detail-hero .detail-sub{display:none}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 3 in 10. We matched 12,125 Horry County home sales to their buyers on the county's tax records. 3,603 of those buyers have their tax bill mailed outside South Carolina.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Buyers whose tax bill goes to another state, Horry County"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Buyers whose tax bill goes to another state, Horry County</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Kind of home, sales counted</span><span role="columnheader">share of buyers</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condos</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">2,402 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.544" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">54%</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Every home sold</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">12,125 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.297" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">30%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Other homes</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">9,723 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.236" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">24%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Home sales of $10,000 or more recorded from October 2025 through September 2026. A buyer who already lives here counts as local, so the true share of buyers from other states is higher.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Condos draw them most. More than half of condo buyers had a tax bill in another state, against about 1 in 4 buyers of other homes.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Where the home is matters too. In the city of North Myrtle Beach, 55% of buyers had an out-of-state tax bill. Inland in Conway, it was about 1 in 10.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list [role=row]{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) auto;column-gap:.8rem;align-items:start}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list .b{display:none}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list .dot{display:none}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list .f{display:block}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list [role=cell]{justify-content:flex-end}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact c3-gchart-list"><div role="table" aria-label="Buyers with an out-of-state tax bill, by town"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Buyers with an out-of-state tax bill, by town</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Town, sales counted</span><span role="columnheader">share of buyers</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">1,856 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.551" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">55%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Surfside Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">175 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.463" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">46%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">1,685 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.453" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">45%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Garden City</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">412 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.393" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">39%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Little River</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">558 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.283" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">28%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Carolina Forest</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">576 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.182" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">18%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Socastee</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">426 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.12" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">12%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Conway</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">450 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">10%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Towns are the Census cities, towns and communities that hold each sold home. Surfside Beach had the fewest sales. Murrells Inlet is mostly outside the Census line of that name, so it is left out.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a condo in North Myrtle Beach or Myrtle Beach, your buyer most likely has a home in another state. About two of every three condo buyers in those two cities did. Chapter3 spends several thousand dollars a month on ads aimed at buyers moving to the Grand Strand, so your listing reaches them where they live now.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The owners around you show the same thing. Across every home in the county, 1 in 4 has its tax bill mailed outside South Carolina. For condos it is nearly 6 in 10.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="pay-more"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Do they pay more">Do out-of-state buyers pay more?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No sign of it. In the same building or subdivision, buyers with an out-of-state tax bill paid the same middle price as South Carolina buyers, and the records show no sign that they pay more. About half of each group paid more than their neighbors' middle price, and half paid less.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Paid more than the middle price of the other sales in the same place"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Paid more than the middle price of the other sales in the same place</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Buyer, resales</span><span role="columnheader">share who paid more</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Buyers with an out-of-state tax bill</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">1,624 resales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.477" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">48%</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Buyers with a South Carolina tax bill</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">1,831 resales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.497" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">50%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>3,455 resales in 316 buildings and subdivisions with at least six sales and a buyer from each side, October 2025 through September 2026. Each price is compared with the middle price of the other sales in the same place.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The old idea is that a buyer from New York sees a bargain and pays up. The first half is true: a typical home here is worth about half of one in Suffolk County, New York. In the records, they still paid what their neighbors paid.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One client household from New York sold a house worth about $1 million that cost more than $20,000 a year in property tax. Here they bought a house of about $700,000, with about $3,200 a year in tax. That is one household, not a promise.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For you as the seller, that means two things. Price the home to what sold near it, the way we would for a local buyer. Then make sure the listing reaches the third of buyers who are not here, and the half of condo buyers.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="where-from"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Where they come from">Where do out-of-state buyers come from?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">North Carolina, by far. Of the 3,603 buyers with an out-of-state tax bill, 916 had one in North Carolina. New York, Pennsylvania, New Jersey and Virginia came next.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list [role=row]{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) auto;column-gap:.8rem;align-items:start}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list .b{display:none}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list .dot{display:none}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list .f{display:block}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-list [role=cell]{justify-content:flex-end}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact c3-gchart-list"><div role="table" aria-label="Where the out-of-state buyers' tax bills go, top states"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Where the out-of-state buyers' tax bills go, top states</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">State</span><span role="columnheader">buyers</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Carolina</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">916</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">New York</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.478" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">438</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Pennsylvania</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.41" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">376</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">New Jersey</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.286" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">262</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Virginia</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.279" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">256</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Maryland</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.184" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">169</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Ohio</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.163" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">149</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Out of 3,603 buyers with a tax bill outside South Carolina, October 2025 through September 2026. 21 had a tax bill in Canada.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our own relocating clients come mostly from New York and North Carolina, the same two states at the top of the county's records.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many people also move here to live. About 14,200 more people moved into Horry County from the rest of the United States than moved out in the year to July 2025. That is about 39 people a day. Deaths outnumber births here, so the county grows because people move in.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Myrtle Beach area grew 3.2 percent that year, faster than every other U.S. metro area but one, out of 387. Over one tax year, 12,877 households moved to Horry County from another state. The largest source outside South Carolina was Suffolk County on Long Island, New York, with 320 households.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Homes and taxes cost much more where many of them come from. A typical owner-occupied home in Horry County is worth about half of one in Suffolk County and about two-fifths of one in Nassau County. Its owner pays less than a tenth of the property tax.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>A typical home its owner lives in, and its property tax</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">County</th><th scope="col" class="n">Typical home value</th><th scope="col" class="n">Typical property tax a year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Horry County, SC</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical home value">$287,700</td><td class="n" data-label="Typical property tax a year">$944</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Suffolk County, NY (Long Island)</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical home value">$578,400</td><td class="n" data-label="Typical property tax a year">$10,000 or more</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Nassau County, NY (Long Island)</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical home value">$684,700</td><td class="n" data-label="Typical property tax a year">$10,000 or more</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Ocean County, NJ</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical home value">$398,400</td><td class="n" data-label="Typical property tax a year">$6,562</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mecklenburg County, NC (Charlotte)</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical home value">$406,800</td><td class="n" data-label="Typical property tax a year">$2,890</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Wake County, NC (Raleigh)</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical home value">$461,300</td><td class="n" data-label="Typical property tax a year">$3,260</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Brunswick County, NC</th><td class="n" data-label="Typical home value">$349,800</td><td class="n" data-label="Typical property tax a year">$1,890</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The low tax here is for a home its owner lives in, which is assessed at 4 percent of market value. A second home or a rental is assessed at 6 percent. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax calculator</a> gives the bill for either.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="when"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="When they buy">Is fall or winter too late to list?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. Out-of-state buyers closed on homes here in every month of the year. The fewest closed in January, 207. The most closed in October, 352, and May, 350.</p><style>main#main .c3-months{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem;max-width:820px;padding:1.2rem 1.25rem 1rem;border-radius:20px;box-sizing:border-box;background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.76),rgba(255,255,255,.52));border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.9),0 18px 40px -26px rgba(28,32,40,.35)}main#main .c3-months .cap{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1.1em;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 1rem}main#main .c3-months ol{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.55rem}@media (min-width:700px){main#main .c3-months ol{grid-template-columns:repeat(6,minmax(0,1fr))}}main#main .c3-months li{margin:0;padding:.6rem .65rem .65rem;border-radius:14px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.62);border:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.08);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.15rem}main#main .c3-months .m{color:var(--muted);font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.2}main#main .c3-months .m b{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}main#main .c3-months .k{color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;line-height:1.1;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}main#main .c3-months .t{display:block;height:.42rem;border-radius:999px;background:rgba(28,32,40,.08);overflow:hidden;margin:.2rem 0 .1rem}main#main .c3-months .t i{display:block;height:100%;width:calc(100% * var(--f));border-radius:999px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,#c4783a,#d4894a)}main#main .c3-months .s{color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-months li.hi{border-color:#c4783a;background:rgba(255,255,255,.85)}main#main .c3-months figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.55;margin:1rem 0 0}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-months{padding:1rem .8rem .9rem}main#main .c3-months li{padding:.55rem .55rem .6rem}main#main .c3-months .k{font-size:1.4rem}}@media (prefers-reduced-transparency:reduce){main#main .c3-months{background:#fbf8f3}}</style><figure class="c3-months"><span class="cap" id="c3-months-cap">Out-of-state buyers' closings, month by month</span><ol aria-labelledby="c3-months-cap"><li class="hi"><span class="m"><b>Oct</b> 2025</span><span class="k">352</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:1"></i></span><span class="s">27% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Nov</b></span><span class="k">279</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.793"></i></span><span class="s">25% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Dec</b></span><span class="k">336</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.955"></i></span><span class="s">27% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Jan</b> 2026</span><span class="k">207</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.588"></i></span><span class="s">24% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Feb</b></span><span class="k">239</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.679"></i></span><span class="s">31% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Mar</b></span><span class="k">290</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.824"></i></span><span class="s">31% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Apr</b></span><span class="k">328</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.932"></i></span><span class="s">34% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>May</b></span><span class="k">350</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.994"></i></span><span class="s">34% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Jun</b></span><span class="k">324</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.92"></i></span><span class="s">32% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Jul</b></span><span class="k">305</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.866"></i></span><span class="s">29% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Aug</b></span><span class="k">310</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.881"></i></span><span class="s">33% of sales</span></li><li><span class="m"><b>Sep</b></span><span class="k">283</span><span class="t" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--f:0.804"></i></span><span class="s">31% of sales</span></li></ol><figcaption>Deeds recorded in Horry County from October 2025 through September 2026, by the month the deed was recorded. The share is of all home sales that month.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Their share of all sales ran from about 1 in 4 in January to about 1 in 3 in April. A deed is recorded at closing, about a month or more after the buyer signs the contract. The 336 buyers who closed in December made their offers in the fall.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A home listed in the fall still meets buyers from other states, so list it when it is ready.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="sell-from-away"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Selling from away">Can you sell a Grand Strand home without coming back?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. You can sign the deed in the state where you live, before a notary there, and the sale closes here without you. Each step below says where you are for it.</p><style>main#main .c3-steps{list-style:none;counter-reset:s;margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem;padding:0;max-width:760px;display:grid;gap:.7rem}main#main .c3-steps>li{counter-increment:s;position:relative;margin:0;padding:.95rem 1.1rem .9rem 3.6rem;border-radius:16px;background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.76),rgba(255,255,255,.52));border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.9),0 10px 26px -20px rgba(28,32,40,.35);color:var(--muted);line-height:1.65;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem)}main#main .c3-steps>li::before{content:counter(s);position:absolute;left:1rem;top:.95rem;width:1.9rem;height:1.9rem;border-radius:50%;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#c4783a,#d4894a);color:#fff;font-weight:700;font-size:1rem;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-family:var(--sans)}main#main .c3-steps strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.15rem}main#main .c3-steps .who{display:inline-block;margin-top:.35rem;font-size:.9rem;color:#8a5226;font-weight:600}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-steps>li{padding:.85rem .9rem .85rem 3.2rem}main#main .c3-steps>li::before{left:.8rem;top:.85rem;width:1.75rem;height:1.75rem}}</style><ol class="c3-steps"><li><strong>We list the home and show it to buyers.</strong>Our agent prices it to what sold near it and shows it to buyers and their agents.<br><span class="who">Where you are: at home</span></li><li><strong>You sign the offer and the contract online.</strong>We send each page to you to sign by e-signature and answer the buyer's agent for you.<br><span class="who">Where you are: at home</span></li><li><strong>The buyer's inspector and appraiser visit the home.</strong>You do not need to be there for either visit.<br><span class="who">Where you are: at home</span></li><li><strong>A South Carolina attorney prepares the closing.</strong>Every home closing here must be supervised by a South Carolina attorney. The attorney's office prepares the deed and the closing papers.<br><span class="who">Who: the closing attorney in Horry County</span></li><li><strong>You sign the deed where you live.</strong>You sign before a notary in your own state, with two witnesses. The notary adds a seal, the state and the date the commission ends. The deed then records in Horry County as if you had signed it here.<br><span class="who">Where you are: a notary near you</span></li><li><strong>The attorney records the deed and sends your money.</strong>The deed is recorded at the Register of Deeds in Conway, and the attorney's office sends you your money after the closing.<br><span class="who">Who: the closing attorney</span></li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Plan to sign in person, with the notary beside you. A South Carolina notary and the signer must be in each other's physical presence, and South Carolina law has no remote, video notarization.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If you would rather someone sign for you</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You can name a person you trust to sign for you, with a power of attorney. In South Carolina it is signed with two witnesses, like a will, and notarized like a deed. A power of attorney made under the law of the state where you sign it can also be valid. It can be recorded in Horry County for $25.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Keep your money safe</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Wire instructions never change by email. Give your bank details only by phone, on a number you agreed on in advance, and never follow instructions in an email. In 2025, people reported $275 million lost to real estate fraud to the FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center, up from $174 million in 2024.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What it costs to record</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina charges a deed recording fee of $1.85 for each $500 of the price, and the seller owes it. On a $300,000 sale, that is $1,110. Our <a href="/sell/net-proceeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">net proceeds calculator</a> adds it to the rest of your closing costs. Real estate commissions are negotiable between you and the brokerage.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One of our agents sold a rented house for an owner who was moving to New York to be near his sister. A cash buyer closed 13 days after it was listed. The whole story is on our page about <a href="/sell/rental-property/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">selling a rental property</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="withholding"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What the state holds back">What does South Carolina hold back at closing when you live out of state?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Part of the price, for South Carolina income tax. When the seller's permanent home is outside South Carolina, the buyer must hold back part of the price, unless the sale is exempt, for example a main home whose gain is inside the federal exclusion. The seller's affidavit, Form I-295, lowers the amount.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>What the buyer holds back when the seller lives in another state</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Your sale</th><th scope="col">What the buyer holds back</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">It was your main home, and the gain is inside the federal exclusion</th><td data-label="What the buyer holds back">Nothing</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You sign the seller's affidavit, Form I-295</th><td data-label="What the buyer holds back">A share of your gain</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You do not sign the affidavit</th><td data-label="What the buyer holds back">A share of the whole price</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The federal exclusion is up to $250,000 of gain, or $500,000 for a married couple filing jointly, if you owned and lived in the home at least 2 of the last 5 years and have not used the exclusion on another home in the 2 years before the sale. On a joint return, both of you must have lived there.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller files a South Carolina return for the year of the sale, and the amount held back counts toward the tax owed there. Our <a href="/sell/capital-gains/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">capital gains and withholding guide</a> has the rate, a worked example and a calculator.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A Canadian owner who is not a U.S. citizen or resident also faces a separate federal hold-back, generally 15 percent of the price. Our page for <a href="/invest/canadian-buyers/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Canadian owners</a> covers it. Selling a house you inherited from far away? Our <a href="/sell/inherited-house/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">inherited house guide</a> covers that sale.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="disclosure"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Your disclosure">What must you tell a buyer about a home you rarely see?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">What you know, and nothing you do not. You give the buyer South Carolina's written disclosure statement, the same as an owner who lives next door. On any item you do not know, you may mark that you make no representation.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">What you do know, you must tell. An owner who knowingly gives false, incomplete or misleading information is liable to the buyer for the damage. The statement must also say if a rental, rental management or vacation rental contract will still be in place at closing. For a condo, it covers your unit, not the building's common areas.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Some sales need no statement: a sale by an estate's personal representative or a trustee, a sale to a spouse, a child or another direct relative, the first sale of a home no one has lived in, or a sale where both sides agree in writing. We go through the form with you before any buyer sees it.</p><div class="c3-story" style="background:var(--ivory);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);padding:1.5rem 1.5rem .7rem;margin:1.4rem 0 1.4rem;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:640px;margin:0 0 .9rem"><strong>Example:</strong> An owner in Charlotte wants to sell the condo she rents out in North Myrtle Beach.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:640px;margin:0 0 .9rem">A Chapter3 agent lists it and shows it to buyers while she stays home. The agent sees that her rental management contract runs past the closing day. The owner names that contract on her disclosure statement. She signs the deed at a notary near her home, with two witnesses.</p></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="selling-from-out-of-state-questions"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Selling from out of state: questions</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I sell my house in South Carolina while living out of state?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. You sign the deed before a notary in your own state, with two witnesses, and it records in Horry County as if you had signed it here. A person you name with a power of attorney can also sign for you.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do I need to be at the closing?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. Every home closing here must be supervised by a South Carolina attorney, whose office records the deed in Conway and sends you your money after the closing.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the South Carolina nonresident withholding?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When the seller's permanent home is outside South Carolina, the buyer must hold back part of the price for state income tax, unless the sale is exempt. With the seller's affidavit, Form I-295, the hold-back is figured on the gain instead of the whole price.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do out-of-state buyers pay more?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No sign of it. In the same building or subdivision, buyers with an out-of-state tax bill paid the same middle price as South Carolina buyers.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How many people move to Myrtle Beach a year?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 14,200 more people moved into Horry County from the rest of the United States than moved out in the year to July 2025, about 39 a day.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is fall a bad time to sell to out-of-state buyers?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. Out-of-state buyers closed every month. October had the most closings, 352, and January the fewest, 207.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deed index</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycounty.org/parcelapp/rest/services/HorryCountyGISApp/MapServer/24" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County parcels</a>, <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census population estimates</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/statistics/soi-tax-stats-migration-data" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS migration data</a>, <a href="https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B25077" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census ACS</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t30c005.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 30-5</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t26c001.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 26-1</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t62c008.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 62-8</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c008.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-8-580</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/sites/dor/files/forms/I295.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Form I-295</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/404mgbzc/prereqs.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Register of Deeds</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc701" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Topic 701</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/firpta-withholding" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS FIRPTA</a>, <a href="https://www.ic3.gov/AnnualReport/Reports/2025_IC3Report.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI IC3 2025</a>, <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/about-us/blog/mortgage-closing-scams-how-protect-yourself-and-your-closing-funds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB</a>. Buyers: home sales the county recorded over 12 months, matched to the buyer's tax-bill address. Not legal or tax advice.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Get a price and a plan for your home</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Tell us the address and where you live. We send what similar homes sold for and how the sale would close from your state.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/sell/#seller-cma-form">Get my price and plan</a></div></section></main>
